# Teensy 4.1 RAM1 overflow — bank quantization

When a firmware build fails with:

```
RAM1: variables:V, code:C, padding:P   free for local variables:-N
Error program exceeds memory space
```

**Do not** guess. RAM1 is 512 KB FlexRAM = **16 banks x 32768**. The linker assigns whole banks to ITCM (code), and DTCM (variables) gets the leftover banks:

```
itcm_banks = ceil((C + P) / 32768)          # (C + P) is already a 32768 multiple
dtcm_free  = (16 - itcm_banks) * 32768 - V  # negative == the -N you see
```

`-N` almost always means **ITCM tipped into one more 32 KB bank**, stealing a bank from DTCM — not that variables grew by `N`.

## Diagnose without rebuilding

The last (even failed) build leaves `firmware.elf`. Analyze it directly:

```bash
NM=~/.platformio/packages/toolchain-gccarmnoneeabi-teensy/bin/arm-none-eabi-nm
ELF=~/Library/Caches/pio-build-250513-215524-teensy41/teensy41-capture-serial/firmware.elf
# DTCM variables = .data (0x2000xxxx) + .bss (0x2001xxxx); .bss.dma (0x2020xxxx) is RAM2, not DTCM.
"$NM" -C -S --radix=d "$ELF" | \
  awk 'NF==4 && ($3~/[bBdD]/) && ($1+0)>=536870912 && ($1+0)<538968064 {printf "%8d  %s\n",$2,$4}' | \
  sort -rn | head -40
```

The top rows are your largest DTCM globals — the relocation targets.

## Fix decision tree

**Prefer freeing DTCM (byte-for-byte payoff):** move large **global class objects** to `DMAMEM` (OCRAM/RAM2, which has ~490 KB free).

- SAFE for class instances with a **real constructor** (it self-initializes at startup).
- UNSAFE for POD structs relying on `{}` / zero-init: `.bss.dma` is `(NOLOAD)` and Teensy startup does **not** zero it.
- NEVER move ISR / MIDI-clock-hot objects (`midiHandler`, `trackManager`, playback state) — DTCM is the fast path.
- Good targets: display/UI caches, storage-session job RAM, load/save list caches.

Example (fixed the -1888 case by freeing 9472 bytes):

```cpp
// src/DisplayManager.cpp
DMAMEM DisplayManager displayManager;  // was: DisplayManager displayManager;
```

**Freeing ITCM (code) rarely helps — unless you move a whole bank's worth.** `FLASHMEM` only recovers a DTCM bank if it drops `(C + P)` below the next lower 32768 multiple. If code is only a few KB into a bank, small `FLASHMEM` moves do nothing. But a *bulk* move does work, and it is the only lever left once every movable DTCM global is already `DMAMEM`: [`linker/imxrt1062_t41_lcr.ld`](../../linker/imxrt1062_t41_lcr.ld) routes named object files' `.text` / `.text.*` into `.text.code` (FLASH). Measured 2026-09-18: routing ten cold TUs (storage / boot / telemetry / undo / browser-read — none in the MIDI tick, the capture append, or playback serving) took `code + padding` 425,984 -> 393,216, i.e. **13 -> 12 ITCM banks**, and returned a full 32,768 bytes to `free for local variables` (6,336 -> 39,104). Three things to know before repeating it:

- A TU's `size -t` text figure overstates what moves: after `--gc-sections` only ~20-30 % of it was actually resident in ITCM. Measure by rebuilding, and iterate — one round may only shrink `padding`.
- `padding` is the slack to the next bank. `padding` is 1,256 after that move, so the next ~1.2 KB of new ITCM code crosses back and costs a whole bank; take the next bank deliberately by moving another ~32 KB of cold code.
- The boot path, not the linker, is what fails. At 13 banks with `variables` at 96,064 the stack had 2,240 bytes and the boot-load overran it **silently** — no crash record, USB still enumerated, serial stopping at `BOOT,scan,start`. A semantically neutral edit reproduced it, so here "a few bytes of new code" can cost 4 KB of DTCM.

**A new capture format string can cost 4 KB of the local allowance.** Adding two
`DebugSessionCapture` emitters with ~90-character format strings (`emitCapPrintf`) moved
`variables` by +4,096 with *no* symbol size change: the extra bytes are `.data`/`.bss`
alignment padding (a `.data` region grew ~180 bytes and the following `ALIGN(1024)`/`ALIGN(4096)`
symbols snapped). Shortening the same tags to `DIAG,reentry_window_open` / `DIAG,canonical_release`
restored the allowance exactly. So when a change adds capture tags, read `free for local
variables` in the build output and try a shorter tag before moving any global.

**A `switch` over an enum costs the same way.** `windowed-playback-generation` slice 3.1
(2026-10-02) added three small switches to a `Track` TU whose functions are already
`FLASHMEM`: their 16–24 B `CSWTCH` jump tables landed in `.data`, `.data` went
77,504 -> 78,528 and `variables` went +4,096 with **no per-object `.data`/`.bss` change at
all** (only `.text` symbols differed). Replacing the switches with an `if`/`else` chain and
dropping a `const char*` name array (also `.data`) restored `variables` to 96,192 exactly.
So: when a change adds a small branch table anywhere, read `free for local variables`
before blaming a new global, and prefer a branch chain or a literal at the reporting site
over a table.

**A readiness-holder name costs the same as a capture tag.** `windowed-playback-generation` slice 3.5
(2026-10-02) added six holder-name literals (`"window_build_owner"` and friends, ~100 B) to
`TrackPlaybackWindowBuild.cpp`. `.rodata` is DTCM in this script, so `variables` went 96,192 -> 100,288
(+4 KB, locals 34,880 -> 30,784) with `code` **byte-identical** — the align-edge mechanism above,
triggered from a *readiness* slice rather than a capture one. The natural first fix is rejected by the
compiler and is worth knowing before spending a build on it: `TRACK_COLD_MEM` on the literals fails with
*"'kReadinessUnknownName' causes a section type conflict with 'ProjectionContext
makePlaybackContext(const Track&, const Loop&, uint32_t)'"*, because that TU's functions already occupy
`.flashmem` as **code** (`*(.flashmem*)` sits inside `.text.code`) and one section cannot hold text and
rodata. The fix that works is the carve-out this script already carries for the TUs whose tags crossed
the same edge: add `*TrackPlaybackWindowBuild.cpp.o(.rodata.str* .rodata.*.str*)` to `.text.progmem`
(> FLASH), which restored `variables` to 96,192 exactly with no re-base. So when a cold TU's *strings*
cross the edge, route them through `.text.progmem` in the script — `.flashmem` is code there.

## Verify

```bash
pio run -e teensy41-capture-serial   # expect: free for local variables >= 0
```

Confirm the math: `(16 - itcm_banks) * 32768 - variables == reported free`.

Related: [`docs/Guides/INTERNAL_HEAP_AND_EXTERNAL_MEMORY.md`](../../docs/Guides/INTERNAL_HEAP_AND_EXTERNAL_MEMORY.md) (heap tiers — a different axis: malloc RAM2 vs PSRAM, not the DTCM/ITCM linker split).
